\emph{The conclusion.} The stored theorems that name a Yukawa coupling or a doublet admixture (besides those used here, for example \claimref{synthesis.no-120-excludes-hierarchical-second-stage}, \claimref{synthesis.complex-neutrino-stages-keep-the-solar-sum-rule}, \claimref{synthesis.residual-s2-decouples-first-family-from-ckm-and-pmns}, \claimref{synthesis.stored-fits-are-models-of-not-u}, \claimref{synthesis.hypothesis-u-readings-on-the-rung-coupling-line}, \claimref{standard-model.equal-weight-module}) hold in every model of their premises, so no list of them is needed: independence follows from the postulates. The postulates of the cone of this result, with those of the relations audited, are (F1)-(F4), the slot-module premise, the indifference premise, the face identification, (EW), (U), (H), (M), the Majorana-scale premise and the real-breaking assumption; (F1)-(F4), the slot-module premise, the indifference premise and the face identification name no coupling, and the others are invariant under $h_{\mu}$ by (iii) (for (U) in its eigenvalue reading with $m_{b}/m_{t}$ given) and hold in the diagonal model of (iv), while the bottom reading on the model's own $m_{b}$ fails alike in the pair A, C that differ in the factor. Hence the $126$ coefficient relative to the $10$ in the down-type sector is not a consequence of the stored relations. What fixes it is the record: $\mathrm{Sym}(M_{d} - M_{e}) = 4w_{d}f$ is a function of the down-quark and charged-lepton mass matrices. At the level of the record the stored fits (Proposition \claimref{standard-model.fits-record-level}, by citation) reproduce all eighteen fitted observables with the $126$'s second-family entry supplying the factor $3$ in some rows and about a tenth of $m_{s}$ in another, so even the record, which does not fix the right-handed rotations, leaves the second-family share of the $126$ open in the fits' parametrisation.

\emph{What the independence costs.} The second family's factor $3$ ($m_{\mu} \approx 3m_{s}$ at $M_{I}$) is an input of the record, not a consequence of the series; equivalently the down-type $126$ weight ($w_{d}f$ against $v_{d}Y$, or $b/a$ on the module) is a fitted number. What stays derived is the Clebsch coefficient: given that the $(15,1,1)$ part enters, its lepton-to-quark ratio is $-3$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.georgi-jarlskog-is-rung-vector-times-pair-part-of-majorana-square}). Results that use the factor as coming from the $126$'s second-family entry (rows two to four of the stored fits) are fits in that entry; under an $S_{3}$-invariant Majorana sector the $126$ cannot give the second family alone a factor (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residual-s2-fixes-120-family-form}(iii)), and the light-plane scalar $4w_{d}\alpha/v_{R}$ is the free number. $h_{\mu}$ also moves $m_{b}/m_{	au}$ at $M_{I}$, which is therefore not derived either. Not proved: that no relation outside the store (a potential for the doublet mixing at the intermediate scale) fixes $w_{d}$; such a relation would be new structure, as for the Majorana scale.
